# broiler-shared-package pin 1
#
# One package of the committed, hash-pinned feed (decision SJL-D-4 (c); milestone SJL-2.7): a shared
# JavaScript library package whose home is the Broiler.JS repository, retained here byte for byte so
# that a restore needs no other repository's folder and no network for it. NuGet.config's package
# source mapping sends the Broiler.ECMAScript prefix to this directory and to nuget.org; the lock
# file of a project that references it records `content-hash`, and rule I1 holds that hash to this
# pin and to the allow-list.
#
# HOW IT WAS TAKEN. The package exactly as the Broiler.JS repository's publish-shared workflow pushed
# it to nuget.org on 2026-10-15 (run 38813070829, its artifact shared-packages), packed from
# `upstream-commit`; the nuspec's repository element records the same commit, which rule I7 reads.
# nuget.org serves the same package with its repository signature (.signature.p7s) added, and NuGet's
# content hash of a signed package leaves the signature out, so a restore from either source records
# `content-hash`, the base64 SHA-512 of this file's bytes. `sha256` is over the same bytes.
#
# (Revised 2026-10-15, when the package was published. Until then this feed held a copy packed locally
# from Broiler.JS e04874d. Between e04874d and the published commit nothing under Broiler.ECMAScript/
# changes, so the library code is the same; the package differs in the commit its nuspec and assembly
# record, the identifiers derived from them, the case of the repository URL, and the branch the nuspec
# names. The paragraph read: "Packed on 2026-10-15 in the Broiler.JS repository,
# from `upstream-commit`, in a clean checkout of it, with `dotnet pack
# Broiler.ECMAScript/Broiler.ECMAScript.Algorithms -c Release -p:ContinuousIntegrationBuild=true`;
# Broiler.ECMAScript/Directory.Build.props sets the version, 0.1.0-preview.4, at that commit. The
# nuspec's repository element records the same commit, which rule I7 reads. `content-hash` is NuGet's:
# the base64 SHA-512 of the .nupkg's bytes, which is what a lock file records. `sha256` is over the same
# bytes. It is not on nuget.org: a Broiler.VM package release that depends on it waits for its
# publication (eng/verify-feed.ps1), and the pin is then taken again from the published package, as
# 0.1.0-preview.3's was." The identity lines read:
#   content-hash ouVhgiEHIzWYuLtmtNEZk9ICTXN966P6KzqvMfYYPVxm4FHDBBBK3ivpc4rJ9kQpPPGRQMhC5RrQv3Oth069ZA==
#   sha256 d04897139c9e81e475abfb180ecc80a925b65c07eeff8e6bf782b4bda4936958
#   upstream-commit e04874d069f10a3f71bbcdd66e497a5b37069368)
#
# WHAT IT IS. The Date area of the shared algorithms library (milestone SJL-6.3; JSD-0072), namespace
# Broiler.ECMAScript.Time, with the number-conversion, numerics and text areas of 0.1.0-preview.3
# unchanged: admitted for PRODUCT use by Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript and
# Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript.Compiler at SJL-6.4, and restored by the admission probe. It carries no
# third-party component beyond 0.1.0-preview.3's; it implements Howard Hinnant's public-domain civil-day
# formulas, which its THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md credits beside those components. It is on nuget.org since
# 2026-10-15, as a Broiler.VM package release that depends on it requires (eng/verify-feed.ps1).

id Broiler.ECMAScript.Algorithms
version 0.1.0-preview.4
file Broiler.ECMAScript.Algorithms.0.1.0-preview.4.nupkg
content-hash fv6d0u0cFEf1HSr59woiaK7zqyfFKDzuBX4FIcTXL/62/E9X7Q3m2/5/+8ao4uz3+x1vSZIH9JLG6UBad3GsxA==
sha256 fccb5cfd107f2122a44bc8d514cb783d9ba0e4482d224a318694127dba1236fe
upstream Broiler-Platform/broiler.js
upstream-commit d1e4b1163b65708ca94257d3654db133f69a833a
